#!/usr/sbin/nft -f
# Only root reaches the metadata service (spec/distribution-requirements/
# L16). On Hetzner Cloud and OpenStack, OVHcloud among them, the metadata
# service at 169.254.169.254 hands out the provisioning data, and with it the
# secrets of kamerplanter: -> secrets:, to whoever asks; OpenStack also
# answers at fe80::a9fe:a9fe. cloud-init, which runs as root, keeps reaching
# it; other local users and the containers do not. Loaded by
# kamerplanter-metadata-guard.service at boot, before the network is up and
# before any container starts, on every target: where there is no metadata
# service the rules match nothing.
#
# The table is the appliance's own. Podman's netavark keeps its rules in a
# table of its own (inet netavark), and firewalld is not installed; a packet
# that one base chain drops or rejects stays dropped whatever another table
# accepts, so the order of the tables does not matter. Reloading this file
# replaces this table and touches no other: the first two lines create it
# when it is missing, so that deleting it never fails, all in one transaction.
#
# output: packets that local processes send. A socket of uid 0 passes; every
# other uid, a rootless container's pasta or slirp4netns process included,
# is refused at once rather than left to time out.
# A rootful container in the host's network namespace (Network=host, or
# rootful pasta) sends with uid 0 and would pass output like cloud-init does.
# That is why every Quadlet of the appliance joins kamerplanter.network, and
# tests/unit/scrub.sh fails on one that does not.
# forward: packets routed through the appliance, which is how the containers
# of the Podman bridge networks (kamerplanter.network) reach the outside: they
# never pass output, and their address is translated only after this hook.
# Nothing is routed to a link-local address for anyone: the whole IPv4 range
# 169.254.0.0/16 stays on its link (RFC 3927), and the containers' DNS goes to
# aardvark-dns at the bridge's gateway, which is not forwarded. So every such
# packet is refused, to the metadata service and to whatever else a cloud
# offers there, an AWS-style DNS or time server among them. Only the replies
# of a tracked connection pass first: those of the containers' published ports
# to a client that itself has a link-local address (a zeroconf LAN, a laptop on
# a direct cable) are routed too, and must reach it. Everything else to that
# range is refused, a packet that conntrack does not track (invalid,
# untracked) included; the accept ends only this chain, no other table.
# IPv6 link-local addresses are not routed either; only the metadata address
# is named there.
table inet kamerplanter_metadata
delete table inet kamerplanter_metadata

table inet kamerplanter_metadata {
    chain output {
        type filter hook output priority filter; policy accept;
        ip daddr 169.254.169.254 meta skuid != 0 counter reject with icmpx admin-prohibited
        ip6 daddr fe80::a9fe:a9fe meta skuid != 0 counter reject with icmpx admin-prohibited
    }

    chain forward {
        type filter hook forward priority filter; policy accept;
        ct direction reply ip daddr 169.254.0.0/16 accept
        ip daddr 169.254.0.0/16 counter reject with icmpx admin-prohibited
        ip6 daddr fe80::a9fe:a9fe counter reject with icmpx admin-prohibited
    }
}
